this is likely just the start of costa  rica's election saga a first round  presidential vote in which a crowded  field of more than two dozen candidates  will be whittled down setting the scene  for a run-off this april  nobody seems to have the 40 percent of  voter support it would take to win  outright analysts say that's because  many of the populists remain  underwhelmed by what's on offer  but there are still front runners let's  have a look  in first place polls lies jose maria  figueres a former president in the 90s  this establishment candidate wants a  second bite and is trading on his  experience to get it although a past  corruption scandal may trip him up  however we see it this country is  falling apart bit by bit i'm offering my  experience in government decision making  and building teams of women and men who  can help us rescue the country and put  it on a different path of green economic  growth jobs combat of poverty and rescue  of national education  second place in the polls lynette  saborio the country's first female vice  president she's hoping that her  scandal-free image and role as ex-head  of costa rica's version of the fbi will  nail her the anti-corruption vote but  she was disappointing in the first  presidential debate and avoided others  we will go hard against impunity we  especially want to put costa rica on the  right path a country that is more just  with more opportunities and fairness for  all people to have the opportunity to  work  in third place there's fabricio alvarado  the surprise package of the elections  four years ago a christian singer with  an anti-gay marriage stance now he's  added infrastructure projects and tax  cuts to his pitch but he's yet to find a  big issue to mobilize his conservative  base this time around  we need to stop governing by hitting the  wallets of costa ricans instead focus on  reactivating the economy and reducing  the sales and salary tax and other  important decisions to take a different  path  but what are people worried by in costa  rica a country which is among the most  prosperous in central america and stable  enough to not even need an army  well there are problems unemployment  lies at almost 20 percent  there's concerns over immigration  especially from haiti and a tourism  industry struggling to recover from the  pandemic  whichever president takes those issues  on they'll need the support of congress  and those seats are up for grabs too  this sunday john holman al jazeera
 

